# GitHub workflow provenance check — TASK-260715-whtdsf

Checked 2026-08-30 against official GitHub documentation.

- A run uses the workflow file from the event-associated commit SHA/ref. A tag
  push therefore cannot itself be the trusted release-policy entry point.
  Source: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/concepts/workflows-and-actions/workflows
- `workflow_run` uses the last commit and ref of the default branch, and its
  workflow file must exist on the default branch. It may receive write tokens
  or secrets even when the upstream workflow did not, so it must never execute
  upstream code or consume upstream caches/artifacts.
  Source: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/events-that-trigger-workflows#workflow_run
- A manually dispatched workflow runs at its selected branch/tag ref, so a
  selectable-ref `workflow_dispatch` is not accepted as the protected release
  entry point.
  Source: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/events-that-trigger-workflows#workflow_dispatch
- Same-repository relative reusable workflows resolve at the caller commit.
  Credential-capable reusable workflows therefore use the explicit
  `owner/repository/.github/workflows/file.yml@<full-commit-SHA>` form; GitHub
  documents full commit SHA as the safest reference.
  Source: https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/reuse-automations/reuse-workflows

Decision: keep tag push only in an unprivileged `release-intake.yml`, and use a
default-branch `workflow_run` `release-prepare.yml` as the trusted entry point.
The trusted workflow independently resolves repository/run metadata and never
uses intake artifacts, caches, checkout, executable output, or asserted verdict.
Unknown/ambiguous metadata fails before candidate acceptance.

